  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This research proposes to test whether media portrayals shape individuals’ beliefs about being alone and whether those beliefs causally moderate how time spent alone changes loneliness, thereby identifying belief-focused interventions for reducing loneliness.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When a social experience varies in emotional consequences, widespread portrayals supply negative meanings, and belief valence is linked to emotional outcomes after that experience, those beliefs become plausible mechanisms to test and practical targets for intervention."];
  "Node_S10" [label="(10,0,0) Currently a study of over 11,000 participants revealed that the strength of the relationship between being alone and feeling alone was weak."];
  "Node_S11" [label="(11,0,0) Currently more recent studies demonstrate similarly modest associations."];
  "Node_S12" [label="(12,0,0) [induction-reasoning]: Given that 85% of time alone is deliberately chosen and American adults intentionally seek out such experiences, therefore time alone can be a desirable part of daily life."];
  "Node_S13" [label="(13,0,0) Currently American adults spend ~30–65% of their waking hours alone and often intentionally seek out such experiences."];
  "Node_S14" [label="(14,0,0) Currently 85% of the time that people spend by themselves is deliberately chosen."];
  "Node_S16" [label="(16,0,0) Currently spending time alone can promote well-being, emotion regulation, and cognitive restoration."];
  "Node_S18" [label="(18,0,0) [induction-reasoning]: Given that spending time alone can promote well-being and time alone can be a desirable part of daily life, therefore there is substantial variability in people's experiences of being alone."];
  "Node_S20" [label="(20,0,0) Currently decades of research indicate that people's beliefs powerfully impact their emotional responses to a vast array of situations."];
  "Node_S21" [label="(21,0,0) [induction-reasoning]: Given that interventions targeting beliefs impact various experiences and research shows beliefs impact emotional responses, therefore the wide-ranging nature of these effects is well documented."];
  "Node_S22" [label="(22,0,0) Currently interventions that target beliefs positively impact experiences that range from stress and depression to weight loss and sleep."];
  "Node_S27" [label="(27,0,0) Currently contemporary U.S. news articles frequently portray being alone as harmful."];
  "Node_S29" [label="(29,0,0) Currently people who hold negative beliefs about being alone experience a significant increase in loneliness after spending time alone, whereas those with positive beliefs experience a decrease."];
  "Node_S3" [label="(3,0,0) Currently media articles and public health campaigns have proliferated with warnings about the dangers of being alone."];
  "Node_S8" [label="(8,0,0) [induction-reasoning]: Given a large study showing a weak relationship and recent studies showing modest associations, therefore being alone and loneliness are distinct states."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) Because belief-targeting interventions can affect diverse experiences and beliefs about being alone are linked to loneliness changes after time spent alone in a harmful-media context, beliefs about being alone are a promising target for interventions aimed at loneliness."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) Time spent alone is not uniformly harmful; it can be deliberately chosen, desirable, and associated with well-being, emotion regulation, or cognitive restoration for some individuals."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) The combination of substantial variability in experiences of being alone and well-documented effects of beliefs on experiences and emotional responses makes belief-related differences a plausible explanatory factor to investigate for why experiences of being alone vary."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Contemporary media and public-health discourse commonly include warnings or portrayals that frame being alone as harmful."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) Evidence that people with negative versus positive beliefs about being alone show opposite loneliness changes after spending time alone, together with widespread harmful media portrayals of being alone, supports studying how media-contextualized beliefs relate to loneliness around time alone."];
